[{:seon.task/name "prove-runs-what-a-change-reaches"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged; AGENTS.md §3 Testing and §5, results reused by input digest; design: docs/source.md §2): one of each. The selection `seon.proofs/plan`: a key runs exactly when no green is stored at its reach digest; with a base (an agent's branch against main at its merge base) it selects the proofs and the moved digests, without one the proofs and every key with no green. The digests `seon.program/reach-digests` (tests by default; bin/check steps by their seon.check functions through `seon.proofs/check-steps`, the one list bin/check reads from `./seon steps`; `seon.proofs/step-digests` names a step whose function is gone as missing, red). The runner `seon.proofs/run-tests!` (bin/test's shards and an agent's test fork). The results directory `:seon.config.source/test-results-dir` (`seon.proofs/greens`, `seon.proofs/store!` through `seon.fx/write-file`). Entry points: `bin/prove` for worktree sessions and root (`bin/test` and `bin/check` without arguments are its halves; `bin/check --steps DIR`), and `seon.agent/test-plan` for an agent's run (the turn's observe, the reconciler's :tests, the merge gate, `(my.agent/prove)`); full runs are explicit (`bin/test --all`, `bin/check 100`); a merge runs bin/prove on the merged tree (docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches, with the measurements). Deleted: `bin/test --reach` and test-runner's plan, run-vars!, reach-main and green?; `seon.agent/test-vars`; `seon.program/reaching-tests`. Shards are processes bin/test starts, not forks of the runner (a forked child of the jolt test runtime hangs at its first d/connect). The digest's inputs: quoted names by namespace, `seon.program/env-readers`, a test's form reached only as its own root, system.edn's procs as data refs (`seon.fx/declared-refs`), a test running the binary only through `seon.test-store/binary-sh`, and the config in force (`test-runner/config-input`). Agents: the source tree's inputs (`seon.fx/inputs`, the one producer, also bin/test's) are read by `seon.fx/read-source` on an effect worker and written on the boot's `:seon.root/source` fact (`seon.root/import-source!`) only when the running binary is built from that tree (`target/seon.tree`, `seon.fx/tree-id`), else `:unshared` says why; an agent uses only its root's current boot's inputs and keeps its greens in the shared results directory (:seon.config.source/test-results-dir) through root's :file owner: the owner ruled (2026-10-23) that a test that is the same is not run again once green, \"especially when freshly branched\", so an agent's greens and a checkout's are one store (seon.agent-test/an-agents-greens-and-a-checkouts-are-one-store). A shard compares the planned tree before storing, so an edit during a run never stores a green under the unedited digests. v1 keyed a result by its reach digest (~/src/seon seon.test.cache). Open: shards are balanced by test count, not time; seon.source-test/a-restart-catches-up-and-a-new-generation-resumes waits on an `nc -w 600` that never times out an idle connection on macOS."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/refs-name-quoted-symbols-and-macro-uses"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/an-unchanged-tree-runs-nothing-and-reuses-everything
                      seon.proofs-test/a-one-defn-change-runs-only-what-reaches-it
                      seon.proofs-test/a-macro-schema-or-config-change-widens-the-selection
                      seon.proofs-test/a-check-step-is-keyed-by-what-its-function-reaches
                      seon.proofs-test/shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored
                      seon.proofs-test/selection-is-proportional-to-what-changed
                      seon.program-test/only-a-test-reading-the-tree-takes-its-digest
                      seon.program-test/the-same-tree-gives-the-same-digests-in-any-checkout-and-any-run
                      seon.program-test/a-test-naming-another-test-does-not-reach-it
                      seon.proofs-test/a-failed-write-or-a-throwing-fixture-is-recorded-red
                      seon.agent-test/a-wide-reach-reuses-unchanged-results}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-judge-reads-run-tests-results"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an agent's run carries clojure.test's report format end to end: `seon.agent/run-tests-here` collects each assertion as a report map (`seon.agent/test-report`), and the judge and the turn read them back through `seon.proofs/results`, `seon.proofs/red-reports`, `seon.proofs/failures`, `seon.proofs/test-run-errors` and `seon.proofs/costs`, while `seon.proofs/run-tests!` already answers one result per test ({:test :digest :pass :fail :error :ms}, a throw's :message and :data). Correct: the judge (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`'s input), the turn (`seon.turn/observe`), the run's facts (:seon.run/failures, :seon.run/costs) and the context read run-tests!'s result map, each red test keeping its failing assertions (expected, actual, got); the report vector, `test-report` and the readers that rebuild a status per test from it go. About -30 lines in src. Proofs: the done error, the run's failures and costs, and the context's red block read the same from the result map as today from the reports."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.proofs/prove-runs-what-a-change-reaches"}}
 {:seon.task/name "turn-logic-from-recorded-data"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: tests shouldn't be running agent turns. A run that names nothing leaves out the tests and steps that reach a process (test-runner/automatic over seon.program/reach-index's :process?; the archived paper process-tests-2026-10-23.md (git history)), so what they cover is proven only when named, in the full run or at root's gate. Where the covered logic is a function of data, test it without a process: the reconciler's step from a recorded fact state (seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc on a db holding the run facts, as procs-test does), the merge from two dbs (seon.merge/three-way, global-ids' delta-three-way), the context render from a recorded basis (seon.context on a :seon.turn/basis), the agent-proc step from recorded events, seon.server/respond on recorded requests, the help page's and templates' forms through seon.repl's handler in process, the restart catch-up as load-all! over a db holding two merges. Each new unit test names the process test whose logic it carries; the process test stays as the end-to-end check, run when named. Measured: the process tests were 90 of 443 tests and 782 of their 917 s (2026-10-23 full run)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-run-that-names-nothing-leaves-out-what-reaches-a-process}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-nested-runs-reports-reach-only-its-run"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.proofs/run-tests!` counts its own tests through its report binding and passes every report on to clojure.test's default report (`seon.proofs/report`, the root value captured at load), so the outer binding is never used. That report bumps whatever tally is in force (Jolt's stdlib clojure/test.clj, `inc-report-counter` and `fail!`): an enclosing t/run-tests' *report-counters* when one is bound, else Jolt's process-wide `counters` atom (`global-tally?`). So a test that runs tests of its own, such as seon.proofs-test/shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored with its deliberate (is (= 1 2)) in app.pv-red-test, adds that fail to the caller's tally. Under t/run-tests the outer test reads red (found by pure-tests, 2026-10-23); under bin/test it is green, because test-runner reads run-tests!'s own counts, but Jolt's process tally (n-fail, failures) still carries the inner fail. Cause: the nested run has no tally of its own. Correct: run-tests! binds clojure.test/*report-counters* to a ref of its own around its test-vars, so a nested run's reports land in that ref and nowhere else (Jolt's global-tally? is false for a caller-bound ref); its results stay its volatiles. Replaces nothing; +1 binding. Proofs: shards-split… is green under t/run-tests as under bin/test, and Jolt's process tally reads the same before and after it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-plan-keeps-a-program-db-per-tree"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23; the owner's option B, docs/source.md). What existed: test-runner/program-db imported the whole of src and test into the in-memory test store on every plan (seon.source/import-tx, then d/db-with): on today's main a plan of tests and steps was 1.94G ticks warm (6.1 s CPU), and transacting the import into LMDB alone 1.43G ticks (4.2 s) even after jolt/datom-cost. The program db is a function of the git tree, so it is kept per tree: one LMDB store per checkout (:seon.config.proofs/program-store, target/program-store), a branch per tree id (seon.fx/tree-id, git's content address, never updated). The same tree reads its branch; a tree never seen is branched from the kept tree with the fewest changed program files (git diff --name-only, trees in the checkout's own repository) and brought current by seon.source/refresh-tx; only an empty store imports the whole program. Every text is read from the tree's git objects (git archive), never the working tree, so an edit after the tree id was taken cannot be kept under it; the diff is --no-renames, so a moved and renamed namespace leaves no old namespace behind. One writer at a time per store (two runs never share a checkout). Bounded in bytes (:seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes): an LMDB file never shrinks, so past the bound the store is removed before it is opened and starts again from the run's tree. A new tree's nearest kept tree costs one git diff per kept tree (a few ms each). Measured: d/branch! 2 ms, a one-file refresh-tx 6 ms and its transact 23 ms; the store 12 MB for the whole program, ~2 MB more a kept tree with one changed file; a kept tree's program db 1.17M ticks and 4.6 MB whatever the program's size (the cost proof); bin/test's selection 1.65 s on an unchanged tree and 1.7 s after a one-file edit, against ~15 s with an empty store. What is left of a plan is the reach graph (~240M ticks, ~1 s). Tests that built a whole program db (program-test, agent-test, main-test) take this process's own store (seon.test-store/program-store). Deletes the per-run import and the runner's note that a fork cannot connect to a store. Option A, a worktree's root answering through seon_prove, stays the end state: this is its tree-to-branch step."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-plan-reads-the-kept-program-db-of-its-tree
                      seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-runs-the-binary-through-binary-sh"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (shrink session): seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-its-keys-under-their-current-names runs bin/ports, which runs ./seon ports, through clojure.java.shell/sh directly, not through seon.test-store/binary-sh, the one call seon.program/reach-index's :binary? counts (:seon.config.source/binary-vars). So bin/test's plan does not build target/seon for it, and on a worktree whose binary is another tree's it is red for a reason that is not the change (config refused: unknown keys). Correct: every test that runs the binary or a script that runs it (bin/root, bin/check, bin/ports, bin/mcp, ./seon) does so through binary-sh, and a law over test/'s forms (read as data) is red on a shell call naming one of them another way, as seon.check-test/every-test-root-starts-through-start-root! is for roots."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-prove-runs-key-does-not-move-between-two-asks"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (owner's ruling 2026-10-24: a prove run is keyed by the digest of what it reads). Cause: main's tx moved between the two asks by the run's own writes, its results and then its prove agent's end facts (status, ended-ms), after the tx seon.prove remembered (:after), so the second ask spawned a second run. A run's key is now branch@digest of what it reads: main's, the checkout's seon.fx/inputs (tree and env) and main's program (seon.program/program-digest, new: it replaces the :after rule, deleted); an agent's, its branch's program digest, which seon.root/prove! and seon.agent/prove! take of the same head (an agent's tx moves too: the eval that asked records itself on its branch). Any file change in the checkout (docs too) or change to main's program moves main's key, and a re-plan reuses every green; coarser than the per-test digests because it must be known before admission, which those cannot be without the run's own planning (spawn and load of the tests, 9-14 s measured). A repeat ask of main: 13.7 s -> 0.48 s. docs/merging.md#the-test-service. Original report: Reproduction: Found 2026-10-23 by the cutover's decider: red in a built scratch worktree of main 4bc598dc1 as well as in the fresh clone, so it is main's, not the cutover's. `bin/test --all seon.prove-test`: `seon.prove-test/a-session-prove-of-main-runs-in-a-prove-agent-and-leaves-facts` (test/seon/prove_test.clj:157-158) asks the same prove twice and expects the same run: the first key is `main@536871112:seon.patterns-test`, the second `main@536871152:seon.patterns-test`, and two run facts exist where one is expected. Cause to find: the key's number (a transaction/entity id of main) moved between the two asks, so the run is not recognised as the same one. Correct: the key names the run by what it is run for (the digest of its inputs), never by a counter that moves when anything else is written; asked again with nothing changed the answer is the same run and nothing runs. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/main-is-proved-at-boot-with-the-config-on-nobody-asking seon.prove-test/a-run-is-keyed-by-what-it-reads seon.prove-test/a-session-and-an-agents-turn-for-one-digest-end-in-one-run seon.program-test/the-program-digest-moves-with-the-program-and-not-with-data seon.program-cost-test/the-program-digest-is-one-read-of-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-law-over-source-runs-in-prove"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the full run of main 888a728e2: three reds (seon.main-test/no-docstring-carries-a-date, seon.main-test/only-measure-reads-the-tick-counter, seon.check-test/a-failed-seed-says-failed) merged because bin/prove leaves out every test seon.program/graph-index flags :process?. The check-test one starts a seed, a real process. The main-test laws only read the compiler's facts of the tree, yet reach one: measured on the reach graph (a BFS from each test's form over :adj to a form in :proc), every law in main-test (also a-runs-lifecycle-is-read-once, a-bound-is-a-config-key) is one edge from `test-runner/program-db`, which calls clojure.java.shell/sh (a :seon.config.source/process-vars var) for `git cat-file`, `rm -rf` of an oversize store and the git archive import (tree-files). A second route, seon.test-store/program-store -> program-store-path's shutdown hook (sh), flags it too. Not the ns form, and not the :reads edge (seon.program/names-only). Every test calling program-db (program-test, proofs-test, test-store, agent-test) is left out for the same reason. Correct: the program db of the tree is built once by the runner's plan (test-runner/tests-of already builds it) and a test reads that value, not a shell; the test's digest still takes the tree (the :reads flag), so a law reruns when the source changes. Splitting program-db into a no-sh open and a shelling import is weaker (a test cannot know the branch exists). Coordinate with the printer session's reach edge (seon.program/names-only, the :reads flag). Proof: bin/prove's plan line counts the main-test laws among the tests to run, not left out; seon.proofs-test/a-run-that-names-nothing-leaves-out-what-reaches-a-process still holds for a test that starts a root."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "mains-verification-goes-first"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's ruling (2026-10-26): main's merge verification goes first, as a fact the tools read, never a rule people remember, a timer or a poll. Seen today: main's merge verification waited an hour for the load to drop, heavy runs queued on a sleep-poll every session copied (the audit's item 10), and merge lines were typed by hand, some green over a red exit (item 5), with process tests named by hand (item 7b). Built: bin/prove --merge holds an exclusive flock on the main checkout's target/main-verify.lock for its whole run; every other bin/test, bin/check and bin/build calls bin/env's main_first, a blocking flock released at once, so it waits only while main's verification runs and says whom it waits for. The command runs the plan's :merge mode (every key with no green at its digest, process keys too), bin/check contracts and smoke, ends on MERGE OK / MERGE FAILED from their exit statuses (merge_verdict) and prints its line for the merge commit's message.md (the counts, the verdicts, the reds, the src delta, the keys left out against main's last line). docs/merging.md#main-goes-first."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-heavy-run-waits-while-mains-verification-runs seon.check-test/a-merges-verdict-is-its-exit-statuses-and-it-writes-its-line}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reads-its-own-recorded-results"
  :seon.task/plan "Answered by the verdict cache (docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache): every run, a turn's, the judge's or a checkout's, writes and reads one cache by digest, so a red the agent's turn just cached is the judge's at the same digest, reused with its failures, and nothing waits on a merge of main. Left: its proof, a judge after a red turn that forks nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-judge-reuses-the-red-its-turn-recorded}} {:seon.task/name "main-is-proved-in-a-fork"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-27: \"We should be able to cheaply (ms) fork main and run main tests and update the shared test knowledge base for all agents.\" What existed: `seon.prove`'s :main input (the writer's commit notice) only kept main's t, so main was proved only when someone asked (a session's seon_prove, root's gate), and an agent forked from main found none of main's results; a main run spawned a prove agent that then read, load-string'ed and imported every test file of the checkout (`seon.agent/load-tests!`, about 2.2 s + 5.4 s cold for src and test, scratchpad tests-fast.md section 6), though the fork server's main already holds the tests (:seon.config.source/dirs takes test/), and main's run was keyed by the checkout's inputs too (`seon.root/prove!` read the tree through the :source effect). Done: a notice whose ops move the program (`seon.program/moves-program?`, moved here from seon.fork-server, which uses it too) is a request to prove main keyed by its program digest, with no waiter, joined by any session asking for that key; the prove agent forked from main runs at once (the :prepare step deleted); main's key is its program digest as an agent's is. Results are the result-tx facts on main every agent forked after reads (`seon.fx/stored-results`). Deletes `seon.agent/load-tests!`, `seon.prove/read-digest`, root/prove!'s :source read, and the answer's `:ran true` that hid the run's own :ran count. Not done: digests equal across bin/test and an agent's run (the reuse session's work); process tests stay with bin/prove --merge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-commit-starts-no-run-of-main-unless-the-config-says-so seon.prove-test/main-is-proved-at-boot-with-the-config-on-nobody-asking}} {:seon.task/name "the-dashboards-run-tests-asks-the-service"
  :seon.task/plan "Left from task seon.proofs/prove-is-the-one-runner: the dashboard's run-tests! call still becomes the agent's :tests op (seon.root/request's table, `run-tests! (cond-> {:op :tests :id id} ...)`, seon.web's /call route), so a run started there is not recorded on main and is not one run with a turn's or a session's of the same head. seon.root/request is pure and holds no digest to key a run by (seon.root/prove! reads the branch's head and seon.program/program-digest). Correct: the web proc's call for seon.root/run-tests! asks the test service as seon.root/run-tests! does (mode :judge, the branch's program digest), its answer delivered to the page as today's. Done: seon.root/request maps the call to the agent's :judge op (the request stays pure: the agent computes its head's digest and asks the service, seon.agent/prove! :judge, as root's judge does), answered as a :tests reply as before. Proof: seon.web-test's run-tests! case asserts {:op :judge}."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/calls-become-requests}} {:seon.task/name "main-run-per-commit-is-off-until-the-child-stops-dying"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-27 (\"disable the test until we can fix it, keep shit moving\"): the automatic run of main's tests at each program commit (task seon.proofs/main-is-proved-in-a-fork) runs all of main's in-process tests, 344 in one forked child, which dies at a varying test (20-60 s in) with exit 1 and nothing on stderr, losing every result: about 200 s of CPU per commit, nothing proved. Off by default behind :seon.config.proofs/prove-main-on-commit; a named run (seon_prove, a turn's, c/prove! with names) is unaffected. On again when a child's death costs only the test it interrupted (each test's reports written as it ends: task seon.agent/a-test-run-streams-its-reports) and its cause is found (the fork's escape, handed to the Jolt session)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-commit-starts-no-run-of-main-unless-the-config-says-so}} {:seon.task/name "an-agent-forked-after-finds-mains-results"
  :seon.task/plan "Found proving task seon.proofs/main-is-proved-in-a-fork (the config on, a root with seon.patterns-test): main's run at boot leaves two result facts (pass) and the greens in the results directory, but an agent forked after has (:reused (seon.agent/test-plan (seon.agent/db) nil #{})) false for both tests, and a named run (c/prove! sys nil '#{seon.patterns-test}) after an unrelated program commit reports :ran 0 :reused 0 :pass 0 (or waits behind the commit's own automatic run). The whole point of proving main is that agents find its greens at their digests (seon.fx/stored-results over the agent's db, and the results directory), so find what differs: the digests the prove agent computes (mode :main, no base, branch :tests) against a fresh agent's, the db the fresh agent holds (does its branch carry main's :seon.proof/* facts at the fork?), or the directory. Reproduce: the test seon.prove-test/main-is-proved-at-boot-with-the-config-on-nobody-asking extended with a spawn! and test-plan on the fresh agent, printing both digest maps and (seon.fx/stored-results db digests). Depends on the reuse session's digest work (tree-readers, :env)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/main-is-proved-at-boot-with-the-config-on-nobody-asking}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-service-reports-what-it-holds"
  :seon.task/plan "Three hangs share one shape (an agent at 0% CPU parked in ```seon.agent/prove!``` for root's answer to its :prove: run 13's agents behind a main run, Phase B's prove agent a2, seon.source-test/a-restart-catches-up-and-a-new-generation-resumes after 71 minutes), and root's stall evidence could not say where the chain waited. ```seon.prove/prove-proc``` answers each waiter once when its run ends (model test seon.prove-test/every-request-is-answered-once-whatever-order-the-api-answers-in: 300 interleavings of requests and answers, errors among them, every request answered exactly once and the service idle at the end), so a waiter that is never answered is waiting on an ask the api never answered, or an answer that never reached the proc's :answers. The service now emits {:op :prove-state :r view} when what it holds changes (```seon.prove/state-view```: the running key, stage and runner, the waiters, the runs waiting and its open asks [step key]); root keeps it (::prove) and a stall task's cause records it beside the api's open asks (:api {:open [[rid kind agent]] :prove view}, ```seon.root/stall-task```), so the next hang says whether an ask is open and to whom. Not found: the ask that is never answered; that is the next hang's evidence. The SIGINFO fiber dump going to the start-time stderr instead of the redirected fd 2 is task jolt.process/a-fiber-dump-follows-the-redirected-stderr."
  :seon.task/kind :whole
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/every-request-is-answered-once-whatever-order-the-api-answers-in seon.prove-test/the-service-reports-what-it-holds-when-that-changes}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-shows-the-results"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's render shows main (seon.render.proc). The verdicts are a cache by digest (docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache), so a render over the one printer (docs/render.md) shows, where root's page shows problems, main's verdicts at its reach digests (seon.fx/stored-results over main's plan): the flaky digests, the reds, and the costliest functions per call over them (seon.proofs/per-call over seon.fx/details). A view renders what is cached, never reruns it (AGENTS §3)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-prove-defers-a-process-test-it-cannot-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7 (an outside agent, 2026-10-27), found proving my.task/status-answers-before-any-turn through seon_eval and seon_prove. A deftest written at the agent's REPL that calls seon.agent-test/in-agent errors in (my.agent/prove) with \"no process store at .../target/runs/test-store-..: bin/test makes it (its plan: make-process-store!) before any shard starts; run the test through bin/test\". (my.agent/tests-reaching 'seon.test-store/shared-root!) lists the new test as reaching in-agent, which is in :seon.config.source/process-vars, yet (:deferred (my.agent/prove)) stayed [] and :at-judge 16: the test ran in the agent's fork instead of being deferred to the merge gate as my.agent/deferred says it is. Find why a test defined at the REPL is not classified a process test (the branch's reach graph not refreshed for a new test? deferral not applied to a registered proof?) and fix it at that producer. Proof: a process test defined on a branch is :deferred by (my.agent/prove), not run and not an error."
